    "question": {
      "dom_id": "question-181771",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,-{n}^{2}+n+12\\le 0}$\n- $B={m\\in Z\\,:\\,-{m}^{2}+m+12\\ge 0}$\n- $C={q\\in Q\\,:\\,-{q}^{2}+q+12\\ge 0}$",
      "worked_solution": "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nLet's start by analyzing the parabola $y=-{x}^{2}+x+12$ for $x\\in R.$\n\nFirst, we find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+x+12 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}-x-12) & =0 \\\\ -(x-4)(x+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=-3$ and $x=4,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14122998/assets/57d58b579557ba0ea8c6179bd37209c8199eee83e17d79c74828a051aa1382d6.png)\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The solution to the inequality $-{x}^{2}+x+12\\le 0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\nx\\le -3\\,\\text{or}\\,x\\ge 4\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{1}=(-\\infty ,-3]\\cup [4,\\infty ).$\n\n- Similarly, the solution to the inequality $-{x}^{2}+x+12\\ge 0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\n-3\\le x\\le 4\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{2}=[-3,4].$\n\nWith these in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $-{n}^{2}+n+12\\le 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{1}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={…,-4,-3}\\cup {4,5,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. If $m\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $-{m}^{2}+m+12\\ge 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB={-3,-2,-1,0,1,2,3,4}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B|=8,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to $-{q}^{2}+q+12\\ge 0$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ and $C$ only.\"",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
      "result": "Incorrect",
